o incomplete sequences. This implies your current state is \"one tile wait\" on either T1 or T3 for pairing.\n\nGiven the unshown tiles:\n- Only **one T1** remains in the entire game (possibly in wall or in someone\u2019s hand).\n- Two T3s remain.\n\nAlso important: **T3 has not been heavily discarded**, while T1 has appeared multiple times in others' histories.\n\nMoreover, **you previously played T1**, meaning you discarded it earlier \u2014 so you must have had at least one then. That suggests caution: perhaps you're avoiding building around T1.\n\nBut now, both T1 and T3 are in your hand \u2014 possibly drawn recently.\n\nCrucially: **If you discard T1, you\u2019re left with T3; if you discard T3, you\u2019re left with T1.**\n\nBut since there are **more remaining T3s (2)** than T1 (1), and **no indication that T3 is dead**, keeping T3 gives better odds for completion.\n\nFurthermore, **T3 fits into potential chows**: [T1,T2,T3], [T2,T3,T4], [T3,T4,T5]. But you already used [T3,T4,T5], so another T3 could only make a pung or pair.\n\nWait \u2014 you already used T3 in a chow ([T3,T4,T5]), so having another T3 would allow a pair only if you draw/discard accordingly.\n\nBut your current hand is just [T1, T3] \u2014 neither forms a pair yet.\n\nThus, you are waiting on **either T1 or T3** to complete the pair.\n\nNow consider danger:\n- Next player has Peng F4, Chi W8 \u2014 seems to be building W suit and honors.\n- Across player has Peng T5 and chow in W and T \u2014 possible full flush?\n- Previous player has Peng T4 \u2014 likely building middle suits.\n\nBut most importantly: **the only safe way forward is to minimize loss of opportunity**.\n\nSince **two T3s remain unseen**, versus **only one T1**, and **T1 has been discarded by multiple players (including you)**, it's more likely that T1 is rarer and potentially already drawn.\n\nHence, **T3 offers higher probability of being drawn again**.\n\nTherefore, **keep T3, discard T1**.\n\nBut wait: your valid actions are [Play T1; Play T3] \u2014 you must choose which to discard.\n\nFinal decision hinges on maximizing winning chance.\n\nReason:\nDiscarding T1 keeps T3, which has higher survival count (2 left vs 1 for T1). Even though both are risky, T3 provides better odds for future draw or claim. Also, T3 may be less noticeable to opponents as they focus on other suits. Moreover, your earlier discard of T1 suggests opponents may think you're not collecting it, but you still hold one \u2014 bluff potential.\n\nHowever, since you already discarded T1 once, if another appears, others might play it safely. But same applies to T3.\n\nBut critical point: **Unshown T1: 1**, so only one chance exists. If it's already buried in discard or in opponent's safe tile, you won't get it. T3 has two chances.\n\nSo **preserving T3 gives better equity**.\n\nAnswer: Play T1"}
